# Same document, same output: stopping markup parsers from drifting apart

Your editor preview looks right. Production does not.

That small mismatch is one of the most expensive failure modes in a markup
stack. The author trusts a JavaScript preview, the server renders with PHP, a
native tool generates the release notes, and each parser makes a slightly
different decision about the same punctuation. Nothing crashes. The document
merely changes meaning somewhere between authoring and publishing.

Carve treats that as a conformance failure, not an unavoidable property of
having implementations in several languages.

## A specification is necessary but not sufficient

A written grammar defines what should happen. It does not prove that two
programs do it.

Differences creep in through ordinary implementation choices:

- one parser normalizes a heading id before resolving a cross-reference;
- another accepts an unterminated fence that its peers leave as prose;
- one sanitizer checks only the first URL in a multi-URL attribute;
- a renderer inserts a newline where another emits adjacent tags;
- an optional feature ships in one ecosystem before the others.

All of those can survive unit tests written independently inside each project.
The tests agree with their local implementation because they inherited the same
assumption.

The useful question is therefore not “does every repository have tests?” It is
“do the repositories test the same observable contract?”

## Use documents and expected output as the contract

Carve keeps a shared conformance corpus alongside the language definition. A
case pairs authored `.crv` with its expected result. Implementations consume
those same cases rather than translating prose into separate local fixtures.

A simplified conceptual case looks like this (the repository's actual shared
fixtures store input and output together in `.test` files):

````text
input.crv
---------
{#install}
## Install

See </#install>.

expected.html
-------------
<section id="install">
  <h2>Install</h2>
  <p>See <a href="#install">Install</a>.</p>
</section>
````

The real corpus covers much less comfortable inputs: nesting boundaries,
unresolved references, malformed fences, spans, table cells, Unicode, output
escaping, and constructs that must deliberately remain literal.

The expected bytes matter. DOM equivalence would allow harmless serialization
differences, but byte comparison buys a stronger operational property: caches,
snapshots, previews, and generated artifacts do not churn when the rendering
runtime changes.

## Share the case, not just its name

Copying fixtures into each implementation creates a new synchronization
problem. A test called `nested-lists` may exist everywhere while containing
three generations of expected output.

The corpus should have one source of truth, and consumers should pin the exact
revision they test. A new language rule then becomes a coordinated change:

. add or update the normative case;
. make each implementation pass it;
. advance the pinned corpus revision;
. release only with the relationship visible.

Pinning is important. Following the corpus's moving main branch would make an
old implementation fail because the language changed overnight. Copying it
would hide that the implementation is old. A recorded revision says precisely
which contract a build claims.

## Test more than HTML

Markup compatibility is broader than one happy-path renderer. Carve's common
implementation surface includes parsing and HTML, Markdown, plain-text, ANSI,
and canonical-Carve output. It also includes lint diagnostics and optional
features where the engine exposes them.

Different targets reveal different bugs:

- HTML exercises structure, attributes, escaping, and URL safety.
- Plain text exposes whether hidden definitions or generated labels leak.
- ANSI output tests presentation without giving the renderer HTML elements to
  lean on.
- Canonical Carve tests whether source can be serialized without changing its
  meaning.
- A second parse after serialization catches output that looks plausible but is
  not structurally stable.

Cross-reading is especially valuable: let implementation A format a document,
then require implementations B and C to parse that result to the same structure.
That detects a private dialect hiding behind an apparently valid formatter.

## Keep safety inside conformance

Security behavior cannot be a note saying “remember to sanitize.” If separate
renderers disagree about a dangerous URL or attribute, choosing a runtime also
chooses a security policy.

Carve makes baseline URL and attribute hardening part of the rendering contract.
Tests exercise denied schemes, event-handler attributes, script-bearing CSS,
invisible bidirectional controls, nesting limits, and output-amplification
bounds. Explicit raw HTML remains a trusted-content feature and must be disabled
for untrusted input, but the protections around ordinary links, images, and
attributes are not optional polish.

The broader lesson is simple: a security statement shared by several
implementations should have shared adversarial fixtures. Otherwise it is a
documentation aspiration.

## Distinguish parity from availability

“All implementations are identical” is usually too broad to be useful.
Packages have language-native APIs, release schedules, and integrations. An
extension may exist in JavaScript before it reaches Rust or PHP. A binding may
wrap the Rust engine rather than implement the parser again.

A precise compatibility claim has three parts:

- *scope:* which syntax tier, renderer, or diagnostic is being compared;
- *version:* which engine and corpus revisions were tested;
- *observation:* semantic equivalence or byte-identical output.

Carve publishes a dated technical snapshot in its
[comparison methodology](https://markup-carve.github.io/carve/implementation-comparison-methodology)
instead of turning a changing test count into a timeless slogan. Users can
choose an engine by runtime while maintainers can see where a release trails the
shared contract.

## What this changes for application architecture

Once parity is continuously tested, a mixed-runtime pipeline becomes less
risky.

A browser can preview with JavaScript while a PHP server publishes the same
source. A Rust command-line tool can validate documents in CI. Python, Ruby, Go,
and WebAssembly consumers can use bindings around the native engine without
inventing another syntax interpretation.

The practical benefit is not the number of language logos. It is that content
stops depending on which component happened to render it.

## Build compatibility as a release property

For any markup format with more than one parser, the pattern is reusable:

. Write normative examples as executable input/output pairs.
. Store them once and pin them from every implementation.
. Compare observable output, including malformed and adversarial source.
. Test serializers by feeding their output to a different parser.
. Publish dated compatibility evidence and name gaps honestly.
. Prevent releases from claiming a corpus revision they did not pass.

That does not eliminate implementation bugs. It turns drift from a user report
into a failed build, which is where the problem is cheapest to solve.

The Carve
[conformance suite](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/tree/main/tests/spec)
and [formal grammar](https://markup-carve.github.io/carve/grammar) are public.
They are useful not only as proof about Carve, but as a concrete example of how
a text format can make “works the same everywhere” falsifiable.
